            <ArticleTitle>Small intestinal angiosarcoma masquerading as an appendiceal abscess.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e22-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1308/003588413X13511609955373</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Angiosarcomas of the small intestine are rare and present non-specifically. They usually manifest with abdominal discomfort, altered bowel habits, anaemia and gastrointestinal bleeding. Diagnosis is often challenging and occurs at an advanced tumour stage. We describe a case of a terminal ileum angiosarcoma masquerading as an appendiceal abscess, and discuss salient clinicopathological features in diagnosing and managing this disease.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
